<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Background tint for the buy-credit segmented toggles (duration + pay rail).
  Checked = the SECONDARY container (the logo's peach arm); unchecked =
  transparent, so "selected" reads from FILL-VERSUS-OUTLINE rather than from
  saturation, and the brand coral stays unique to the one primary action on the
  screen (Continue).

  This is exactly the role secondaryContainer exists for — a supporting tonal
  ground, never a button fill — and Material's own segmented button is tonal for
  the same reason. Two things it must NOT be, both tried and reverted:

  - The default outlined MaterialButton checked state, a colorPrimary overlay:
    a loud coral block indistinguishable from the CTA.
  - @color/chip_checked_container, the checked FILTER CHIP tone. A chip sits on
    a list rail with no primary action to be subordinate to, so it can afford
    the full brand; this control cannot. Wearing it put the segment dE 8.2
    (light) / 5.9 (dark) from the Continue button — close enough to read as one
    colour, which is how the screen ended up as three identical coral blocks.

  Both themes now use the SAME SHAPE — a light warm fill under the dark #460005
  ink — so this control reads as one thing across themes. It did not always: the
  dark value went #FFA8A0 (L* 77, a light fill measuring 10.04:1 against the page
  while the CTA was 6.44:1 — the mode selector out-shouting the button it feeds)
  -> #5C3A22 (a dark container, but under-saturated to half its available chroma,
  so it read BROWN) -> #793A0D (chroma fixed, still read RUST and still looked
  unrelated to light theme) -> #CF743B. Full reasoning on
  md_theme_secondaryContainer in values-night/colors.xml.

  Current separation from the CTA: dE 39.3 light, 28.7 dark. The dark figure is
  the deliberate cost of matching light theme — moving the fill toward the peach
  moves it toward the CTA in lightness. It is still a hue AND lightness
  difference, and far from the dE 8.2 that once made this screen read as three
  identical coral blocks, but it is why the exact light value cannot be used
  here: at 11.64:1 it would out-shout the CTA all over again.

  The stroke stays @color/buy_segment_stroke (outlineVariant) in every state, so
  the group keeps reading as one segmented unit.
-->
<selector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:state_checked="true" android:color="?attr/colorSecondaryContainer" />
    <item android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
</selector>
